Now I have the complete picture, from insertion to extraction to public access. Here's how `:name`-style params are captured, in three stages.

## 1. At registration: the param node is created (`tree.go`, `insertChild`)

When a route like `/user/:name` is registered, `(n *node) insertChild(path, fullPath, handlers)` (`tree.go:288`) scans the path with `findWildcard` (`tree.go:253`), which returns the substring from `:` (or `*`) up to the next `/` or end-of-string — e.g. `:name`.

For a `:` wildcard (line ~310):
```go
if wildcard[0] == ':' { // param
    ...
    child := &node{
        nType:    param,
        path:     wildcard,   // literally stores ":name", colon included
        fullPath: fullPath,
    }
    n.addChild(child)
    n.wildChild = true
    n = child
    ...
```
So the param node's `path` field is stored **with the leading colon** (`":name"`), and the parent is flagged `wildChild = true`. This is why later, when extracting the value, the key is recovered via `n.path[1:]` (strip the colon) — seen in `getValue`'s `param` case:
```go
(*value.params)[i] = Param{
    Key:   n.path[1:],   // "name"
    Value: val,
}
```
(For `*catchAll` wildcards it's `n.path[2:]` because catch-all nodes store path as e.g. `"/*filepath"` — two chars of prefix to strip.)

## 2. At request time: value extraction during tree walk (`tree.go`, `getValue`, param case ~line 480)

`(n *node) getValue(path string, params *Params, ...)` walks the tree matching static prefixes byte-by-byte via `n.indices`. When it can't find a matching static child but `n.wildChild` is true, it jumps to the wildcard child (`n = n.children[len(n.children)-1]`, param/catchAll nodes are always kept last) and increments `globalParamsCount`:

```go
case param:
